{
  "title": "Scope iOS shopping and tag caches to the active account",
  "description": "The iOS recipe cache is scoped by server URL and username, \
but the shopping-list and tag caches are global. `ShoppingItem` has no account \
key, `ShoppingListStore` uses fixed UserDefaults keys for the sync cursor and \
category order, and `TagFilterCache` uses fixed keys for selected and \
available tags. Logout clears credentials without clearing or switching these \
stores. After signing into another user or server, the app can display the \
prior account's cached state and can submit its pending shopping mutations to \
the new account.\n\
\n\
Scope:\n\
- Add an account identifier to locally persisted shopping rows and filter \
every shopping fetch/mutation/sync operation by the active account. Follow the \
existing RecipeCacheStore account-key convention unless a shared account-key \
helper is cleaner.\n\
- Scope the shopping sync cursor, category order, selected tags, and available \
tags to the same account.\n\
- Handle account changes and logout explicitly so stale published state is not \
visible while logged out or after switching accounts. Preserve data per \
account rather than deleting it.\n\
- Add migration coverage for existing unscoped Core Data rows and iOS tests \
proving that two users and two servers cannot see or sync one another's cached \
state.\n\
\n\
This is intentionally iOS-only: the web shopping cache is already keyed by the \
active token.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 1,
